    <origin>Row 10 of task 083's container-selection census. SEVERITY REVISED UPWARD 2026-08-30 on re-verification: the census recorded "three routes with a missing admin gate"; there are NINE, and what they miss is both the admin-role filter and the tenant-scope filter.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Nine routes under the `/api/spe/containers/...` prefix are registered on the **root app**, not on the `/api/spe` group that carries the SPE-admin role filter and the tenant-scope filter. They therefore look like admin routes, sit at admin URLs, and are reachable by any authenticated caller — with `configId` as a bearer capability across tenants.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE BFF security engineer.</role>

  <goal>Every `/api/spe/*` route is subject to the same two authorization layers. No route inherits the admin URL prefix without the admin gate.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>VERIFIED AT CURRENT HEAD, 2026-08-30. Read this section as evidence, then re-verify — do not trust the line numbers alone.

`Api/SpeAdminEndpoints.cs:27-35` builds the admin group with THREE things:
```
app.MapGroup("/api/spe")
   .RequireAuthorization()
   .AddSpeAdminAuthorizationFilter()   // layer 1: is the caller an SPE admin at all?
   .AddSpeAdminTenantScopeFilter()     // layer 2: WHICH customers' data may that admin reach?
```
Eighteen child endpoint groups are registered **on that group** (`:39-58`) and inherit both filters.

`Infrastructure/DI/EndpointMappingExtensions.cs:380` registers `app.MapContainerItemEndpoints()` on the **root app** instead — the comment at `:377-379` explains why ("ContainerItemEndpoints maps absolute paths, not relative to the /api/spe group") and then states the consequence incompletely: *"Inherits auth via RequireAuthorization()"*. It inherits **authentication**. It does not inherit either authorization layer.

The nine routes, each with bare `.RequireAuthorization()` and a client-supplied `configId` query parameter (`Api/SpeAdmin/ContainerItemEndpoints.cs`):

| Line | Route | Effect if reachable |
|---|---|---|
| 36 | `GET  /api/spe/containers/{id}/items` | enumerate any container |
| 51 | `GET  …/items/{itemId}/versions` | version history |
| 66 | `GET  …/items/{itemId}/thumbnails` | content preview |
| 81 | `POST …/items/{itemId}/share` | **mints a sharing link** |
| 97 | `GET  …/items/{itemId}/content` | **downloads the file** |
| 112 | `GET  …/items/{itemId}/preview` | renders the file |
| 127 | `DELETE …/items/{itemId}` | **destroys the file** |
| 142 | `POST …/containers/{id}/folders` | creates a folder |
| 160 | `POST …/items/upload` | **writes bytes** |

`AddSpeAdminAuthorizationFilter` requires the `Admin` or `SystemAdmin` **app role** on the BFF registration (`Api/Filters/SpeAdminAuthorizationFilter.cs:103-106`). No `DefaultPolicy`/`FallbackPolicy` override exists — `AuthorizationModule.cs:231` adds only named policies — so bare `.RequireAuthorization()` means *authenticated*, nothing more.

THE PART WORTH READING TWICE. `SpeAdminTenantScopeFilter`'s own doc comment (`:36-40`) says:

> *"Fifteen endpoint files accept `configId`. A check written into each is a check that will be missed on the sixteenth — and the failure mode is silent cross-customer disclosure, which no test would notice unless it was written to look for it. **One filter on the group cannot be forgotten.**"*

`ContainerItemEndpoints` is the sixteenth file. The filter that "cannot be forgotten" was not forgotten in handler code — it was bypassed by **route registration**, a channel the comment did not anticipate. So `configId` remains exactly the bearer capability that filter was written to abolish, on the nine routes above.

Corroborating signal: `SpeAdminEndpoints.cs:48` carries `// group.MapItemEndpoints();` commented out — someone intended this surface to live on the group.

WHY FOUR PRIOR RECOUNTS MISSED IT. `RouteAuthorizationGuardTests` governs a hand-maintained census of 12 files and `ContainerItemEndpoints.cs` is not among them. Task 083's inverted guard found the file by scanning for SPE write sinks — which is why it surfaced only the three *write* routes (`upload`, `delete`, `folders`). The six read routes were invisible to a write-sink scan and were found only by reading the registration.

SCOPE NOTE. The container-selection defect (client names the container) is real here but is the SMALLER half. The primary defect is the missing admin gate plus the missing tenant scope. Fix the gate first; container provenance follows from it.</background>
