  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Re-verify the registration at current HEAD: that `MapContainerItemEndpoints` is still called on the root app, that the group still carries both filters, and that no fallback policy has been introduced. All three are load-bearing for the severity claim — if any has changed, re-scope before proceeding.</step>
    <step order="1">Prove reachability empirically rather than by reading: a test caller holding NO admin app role should today reach at least one of the nine routes. If it does not, find what actually stops it and correct this POML before changing anything.</step>
    <step order="2">Inventory the SPE Admin client callers of all nine routes (grep evidence), including how `configId` is obtained client-side.</step>
    <step order="3">Move the registration onto the `/api/spe` group (converting absolute paths to group-relative), or — with evidence that this is impossible — attach both filters explicitly. Prefer the former.</step>
    <step order="4">Tests: a non-admin authenticated caller is refused on every one of the nine routes (parameterized over the route table, so route number ten inherits the test); an admin naming another tenant's `configId` gets 404; the shipped admin client still works.</step>
    <step order="5">Add the nine routes to `RouteAuthorizationGuardTests`' census. Perturbation-check: remove the gate and confirm the guard reddens naming the route.</step>
    <step order="6">Reclassify the three write sinks in `SpeWriteSinkContainerProvenanceGuardTests` and correct the corresponding rows in `notes/task-083-sink-inventory.md`.</step>
    <step order="7">Build, tests, publish size vs the 44.96 MB baseline (ceiling 60), CVE scan. Placement Justification per CLAUDE.md §10.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If the SPE Admin client is used by anyone who does NOT hold the `Admin`/`SystemAdmin` app role, STOP. Gating then breaks a shipped surface, and the revert reopens all nine routes. Surface the role-assignment question instead of choosing a weaker gate.</trigger>
    <trigger>If moving to the group changes any route's URL, STOP and surface it — a silently renamed admin route is a broken client, and the fix will be blamed for it.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>Both filters already exist and are already applied to eighteen sibling endpoint groups on `/api/spe`.</existing>
    <extension>This is a registration correction — moving nine routes under gates that already exist. No new filter, no new policy, no new component.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Any authenticated caller — no admin role required — can enumerate, download, preview, mint a sharing link for, and DELETE items in any SPE container they can name, and upload bytes into it, across tenant boundaries, because `configId` arrives from the client and nothing checks whose it is. The sharing-link route makes the disclosure durable and reshareable; the delete route makes it destructive. In SPE these are additive-only permissions: content exposed in a shared container cannot be un-exposed after the fact.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

