  <notes>COMPLETED 2026-08-30. Full write-up: notes/task-091-spe-admin-container-items.md.

Fixed by moving all nine routes onto the `/api/spe` group (group-relative paths; URLs byte-identical,
proven by the tests returning 403 rather than 404). Per-route `.RequireAuthorization()` removed as
inherited. `MapContainerItemEndpoints` now takes `RouteGroupBuilder` instead of `IEndpointRouteBuilder`,
which makes the original root-app registration a COMPILE ERROR rather than a silent hole.

EMPIRICALLY PROVEN BEFORE THE FIX (POML step 1). The new HTTP-level suite, written to assert the correct
behaviour and run first: 9 failed / 11 passed. The nine answered **500/400 from inside the handler** —
never 403 — while the control `/api/spe/containertypes` DID 403 for the same caller. Authorization never
ran; the handler did. Neither of the two obvious instruments could have shown this: the sibling filter
test invokes the filter directly (the filter was never attached), and endpoint reflection cannot see
filters at all (`AddEndpointFilter` contributes nothing to `EndpointBuilder.Metadata`).

Perturbations, both bit: (A) remove the group's role filter → 10 fail (nine + control). (B) attempt the
root-app registration → compile error.

ESCALATION TRIGGER DID NOT FIRE. `SpeAdminApp` is the only consumer and already makes 64 `/spe/*` calls
against group-gated routes, so its users must already hold `Admin`/`SystemAdmin`. Gating adds no new
role requirement.

TWO THINGS THIS TASK DID NOT FIX, both recorded rather than silently absorbed:

(1) Pre-existing client/server route mismatches in `speApiClient.ts` — `createSharingLink` posts to
    `…/sharing` while the server serves `/share`, and `get` calls `GET …/items/{itemId}` which has no
    server route. Both 404, both dead since written, both unrelated to this change. They want a task.

(2) An OPEN MODELLING QUESTION. The three write sinks stay `ClientSupplied`: this task closed the
    authorization half but did not change provenance — an admin still names the container, because that
    IS the function of an admin tool, and record-less containers legitimately exist (078). The guard
    documents `ClientSupplied` as "a work list that shrinks to zero, never exemptions", which may be
    unreachable for an administrative surface. Owner must choose: accept as client-supplied-by-design,
    or add an "administrative, role + tenant scoped" provenance. 091 deliberately did NOT choose.

CENSUS: added to `RouteAuthorizationGuardTests` under a NEW `Scope.GroupGated` + Rule E
(`GroupGatedFilesRegisterNoAbsolutePaths`). A third scope was necessary, not tidy — `RouteLevelGate`
would make Rule A demand a per-route filter that correctly is not there, and a guard that flags the code
it protects gets deleted rather than obeyed (tests/CLAUDE.md).

⚠️ THE POML'S OWN COUNT WAS WRONG AND THE REASON MATTERS. This task was filed after revising 083's "three
routes" upward to nine. 083 said three because its instrument scanned for WRITE sinks; the six read
routes — including file download and sharing-link minting — were invisible to it. A tool finds what it
was built to look for, and the count it returns is not the size of the problem.

Verification: build 0 warnings; new suite 29/29; full BFF suite 10,896 passed / 0 failed / 72 skipped;
ArchTests 138 passed / 6 failed == clean-tree baseline proven by `git stash` (137/6, +1 Rule E); publish
45.12 MB compressed incl. PDBs (0.00 delta, ceiling 60); no vulnerable packages; none added.

---

Row 10 of the task 083 census, at revised severity.

WHAT THE CENSUS GOT WRONG, recorded because the correction is the lesson: it described "three routes (`upload`, `delete`, `folders`) whose primary defect is a missing admin gate." Three was the count of SPE **write sinks** in the file, because the instrument that found it was a write-sink scanner. The file has **nine** routes and the six read routes — including file download and sharing-link creation — were invisible to that instrument. A tool finds what it was built to look for; the count it returns is not the size of the problem.

The second lesson is in `SpeAdminTenantScopeFilter`'s own comment. It correctly predicted that a per-handler check would be missed on the sixteenth file, and correctly concluded that a group filter "cannot be forgotten". Both halves were right. The hole opened through a third channel neither half covered: a file that never joined the group. A control that cannot be forgotten can still be **bypassed by not being applied**.</notes>
</task>
